  "articleBody": "When you're tackling a bathroom waterproofing project, there are a few common pitfalls that can lead to big headaches and costly repairs down the road. First off, a huge mistake is thinking that just tile and grout are enough to keep water out. Nope! They're not waterproof, and you absolutely need a proper membrane system behind them. Another common slip-up is treating waterproofing like a quick DIY task. Rushing the application or using materials not meant for wet areas, like standard drywall in a shower, is a recipe for disaster. You also don't want to just waterproof the seams or corners; the entire wet-area surface needs to be covered to prevent water from sneaking in. Critical spots like floor-to-wall junctions, curbs, drains, and shower niches are often overlooked, becoming prime leak points. Skipping a flood test on the shower pan after the membrane cures is also a big no-no – it's your chance to catch any hidden defects before they become a nightmare. Finally, poor slope to the drain can lead to standing water, stressing your membrane and causing moisture issues. Avoiding these mistakes means your bathroom stays dry and healthy for years to come. If you're unsure about any of these steps, it's a good idea to chat with a professional who specializes in bathroom remodels.",
